hi there Steve Kaufman Here I am in our  little yard here we're in Palm Springs  my wife and I and so I want to talk  about travel and language learning three  questions and people have asked me to  come you know present my conclusions at  the front so they don't have to listen  to my ramble  three questions number one is travel a  major motivator for language learning  yes absolutely second question is it  necessary to travel to the country in  order to learn languages  no third question if you travel to the  country where the language is spoken  will that help your language learning  not necessarily so those are the  conclusions now let's get into detail  for me Travel has always been or has  been one of the main motivators in  language when it got to be careful that  Carmen doesn't trip over a flower or  soup acts out here there are other  motivators like I'm motivated to learn  about a country's history having a  friend in the language can be a major  motivator or a relative so there could  be many motivations or motivating  factors however travel is a major  motivator so having a goal like in my  case when I learned Czech I wanted to  study it for a year and then after a  year go to Prague and speak so that was  a major motivator it made it very  concrete I had a specific goal so to  that extent wanting to travel to the  country even if you can't travel this is  the other thing if you're located  somewhere where you have perhaps only a  slight possibility of traveling to a  spanish-speaking country or to China you  still have thought that one day you  might travel there can become a  motivator because motivation is so  important to language learning so travel  can be a major motivator but it needn't  be the only one as I say it can be an  interest in the culture in say anime for  people I'm not interested in anime at  all but some people are I'm interested  in history so travel can be a great  motivator now the second question is do  you need to travel to the country in  order to learn the language there the  answer is no and I'll give you a number  of examples first of all in my own  experience  I learned Mandarin Chinese in Hong Kong  Hong Kong in the 60s was not a Mandarin  speaking environment so I was not  surrounded by Mandarin I didn't hear  Mandarin on the radio I couldn't go out  and talk to shopkeepers or our  passers-by in Mandarin the only people  with whom I spoke Mandarin were my  teachers and a very limited number of  men  commander and speaking friends whom we  met so I might just as well have been in  New York Paris Tokyo so for me I didn't  need to be in a Mandarin speaking  environment and after nine months I  learned a lot of Mandarin but I was  motivated by my interest in the history  and the literature my reading my  listening and all the things that kept  me going at my Mandarin Studies I get  another example even with regard to  check I spent a year listening to check  radio an hour an hour and a half a day  studying it linked with the goal of  going to Prague but in the end I spent  five days in Prague I spent a year  working on my check so the five days in  Prague were an opportunity for me to  sort of convert my somewhat passive  learning into more active learning but  in terms of the amount of time that I  spent I spent a whole year studying  check on my own in Canada five days in  Prague so being in Prague per se was not  the condition for learning so that's the  second question the third question is  does going to the country ensure that  you learn the language and there again  the answer is no most people if they  have an opportunity to travel to you  know Mexico Spain Italy Prague China  probably they're only going to spend a  week or two weeks there they can't learn  the language in one or two weeks even if  they spend two or three months there it  may not be enough it's the amount of  study that you do beforehand that will  determine how well you can take  advantage of your stay even living in  the country doesn't ensure you're gonna  learn the language when I lived in Japan  very few foreigners learned learned  Japanese and for that matter  you know even if you're in the country  where the language is spoken even and  and I went to Japan's but speaking  essentially no Japanese so I was  surrounded by Japanese people but most  of my learning activity was listening  and reading to things of interest to me  I had to build up my capability in the  language before I could actually  interact with people so regardless of  whether you're in the country or not the  strategy has to be and I mentioned this  in my book the strategy has to be to  build up your own language world of  things that are of interest to you  things that you can read and listen to  and build up your vocabulary allow your  brain to become sort of accustomed to  the language start speaking with people  which we can do it and this is another  point today with the internet access to  people via Skype access to tremendous  resources on the Internet you can build  you can create your own language  learning or language world wherever you  are and that's the major sort of  activity and if you are lucky enough to  have the opportunity to travel to the  country where the language you're  learning is spoken so much the better  that can be a major motivator as you  prepare yourself for that opportunity  and eventually if you do go there  obviously the more time you spend there  the more you're interacting with people  the better you're going to get but the  foundation can be built at home and then  when you get the opportunity you can  really take advantage so that's kind of  a summary of this whole issue of travel  and language yes it's great to travel  and I love going to countries where I  can speak the language and it's a major  benefit and bonus and reward for  learning the language but it's not a  condition you can learn the language  very well at home and even if you do go  to the country where the language is  spoken if you haven't had if you haven't  put that effort into preparing yourself  using your own language world you won't  be able to take advantage of it so there  you have it unfortunately we are going  to have to leave sunny as California in  a few days and go back to Canada where  the weather is not as nice thanks for  listening I look forward to your  comments and I want to hear about your  experience  thank you
 

